#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176612} :activity/id #uuid "f441e707-bca4-5383-a476-aeddd2c81a22" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048144 :topic/id #uuid "82f08561-a294-5382-8640-2c8beeb6ee2e" :topic/math-academy-id 3555} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177094 :tutorial/content "Consider the rational equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{x+3} = \\dfrac{2}{x-2}\n$$\n\nRecall that when the equation consists of a single rational expression on each side, like the one above, then the easiest way to solve it is usually to use cross-multiplication.\n\nHowever, before we start, note that $x=-3$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions. This is because when $x=-3$, we get a zero in the denominator on the left-hand side, and when $x=2$, we get a zero in the denominator on the right-hand side.\n\nWith this in mind, let's solve the equation. First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{12}{x + 3} &= \\frac{2}{x - 2} \\\\\n12 \\cdot (x - 2) &= (x + 3) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n12x - 24 &= 2x + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x - 24 &= 2x + 6 \\\\\n12x - 24 - 2x &= 2x + 6 - 2x \\\\\n10x - 24 &= 6 \\\\\n10x - 24 + 24 &= 6 + 24 \\\\\n10x &= 30 \\\\\n\\frac{10x}{10} &= \\frac{30}{10} \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=3$ is different from $x=-3$ and $x=2$, we accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=3$." :tutorial/id #uuid "32ce7197-6c97-5ece-b45d-186993605c4f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8781 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ecfccb89-86a2-557a-b9d3-c0ad225826b2" :step/math-academy-id 143532 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175906 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175733 :question/id #uuid "1be25af9-d59f-5270-b9e1-d0c5dcd731e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14143"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e09